# Line endings and binary safety.
#
# This file exists for one machine: a Windows laptop running `/plugin
# marketplace add`, which is a git clone performed with whatever settings the
# Git for Windows installer left behind. Its default is core.autocrlf=true --
# check out CRLF, commit LF -- so without the rules below, the clone rewrites
# the launcher and corrupts the binaries, and the plugin fails on the one
# platform it was hardest to test.

# bin/growisto-hook is the file Claude Code actually executes, through Git Bash.
# bash does not tolerate carriage returns: with CRLF, TARGET picks up a trailing
# \r from the uname parsing, HOOK_BIN points at a path that does not exist, and
# the script dies before the code that writes telemetry.log or the NO_BINARY
# marker ever runs. Total silence, and nothing local to read.
bin/growisto-hook text eol=lf
*.sh             text eol=lf

# The committed binaries must reach the machine byte for byte. Git's automatic
# binary detection is usually right, but "usually" is not a property worth
# relying on for a file that has to execute -- and a mangled binary fails the
# same silent way.
#
# Note the dash: this pattern deliberately does NOT match bin/growisto-hook
# above, which is a text file and needs the LF rule instead. That is the same
# distinction build.sh's chmod hint gets wrong in the other direction.
bin/growisto-hook-* binary

# Everything else is text and its line endings do not matter. Naming them
# anyway keeps a future .go or .json file from inheriting a surprise.
*.go   text
*.md   text
*.json text
*.yml  text
